WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:06.000
Working for the SCP Foundation can be a frightening
endeavor, but not all the anomalies they encounter

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.990
are violent, vicious monsters all intent on
destruction.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:15.759
SCPs can often be as lost, lonely and sad
as the rest of us sometimes feel.

00:00:15.759 --> 00:00:20.610
In that spirit, let’s take a heart-breaking
and tear-jerking journey through some of the

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:24.440
saddest SCPs, that might even make you cry.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.529
Get those tissues ready and don’t say we
didn’t warn you.

00:00:28.529 --> 00:00:34.379
To the untrained observer, SCP-348 is just
an ordinary white bowl, with a blue flower

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:37.410
pattern painted on its ceramic surface.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.989
The Chinese lettering on its side translates
to the phrase ‘thinking of you’.

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:47.021
However, if anyone suffering from a minor
injury – like a scraped knee, a cut, or

00:00:47.021 --> 00:00:52.380
a bruise – or any other mild ailment is
presented with the bowl, its anomalous properties

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.260
will begin to show.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:01:00.649
SCP-348 will fill itself with soup, usually
varying in ingredients, but consistently enjoyed

00:01:00.649 --> 00:01:03.039
by those that have eaten from it.

00:01:03.039 --> 00:01:06.860
Finishing the soup, though, that’s what
can start the waterworks.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:13.150
After someone consumes the contents of SCP-348,
a message will appear within the bowl, seemingly

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:15.930
coming from the subject’s own father.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:20.240
In one such instance, a young boy of six,
who had been recently injured whilst playing

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.190
with his friends, was given SCP-348 to eat
from.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:28.000
The boy’s parents were divorced, and his
father no longer living at home.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.030
When the child ate the soup, the message in
the bowl read ‘I’m sorry, son.’

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:38.430
Another similar test was run by the Foundation
using SCP-348, this time with an older boy

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.369
of eleven years old, who was suffering from
a cold.

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:46.270
He had also been adopted by a foster family
prior, and the message that appeared after

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:49.620
he had eaten said ‘I’m glad you’re happy.’

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:54.960
When testing the effects of SCP-348 on adults,
less of them felt inclined to finish the soup

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.009
in the bowl.

00:01:56.009 --> 00:02:01.360
One of these much older subjects, a forty-year-old
woman with a sore throat, had become estranged

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:03.939
from her parents after moving away from home.

00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:08.530
However, she would frequently send them money
and eventually helped arrange senior housing

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.910
for her parents, until her father passed away.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:16.069
While she initially remarked that her soup
tasted bitter, it was fulfilling by the end

00:02:16.069 --> 00:02:17.080
of the meal.

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:21.220
The note left by SCP-348 simply said ‘Thank
you.’

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:26.910
Once, a test was conducted involving SCP-348
and a sixty-year-old man who was suffering

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.660
from a terminal illness.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:33.620
The grandfather to many grandchildren was
offered the bowl of soup and delightedly ate

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:36.510
it, claiming it was the best soup he had ever
tasted.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:42.060
It gave him a sense of satisfaction and even
eased some of the pain caused by his illness.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.920
A week later, the old man passed away peacefully
in his sleep.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:46.970
Oh, what’s that?

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:50.970
Those sweet soup messages weren’t wholesome
enough to encourage even a few sobs.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:56.500
Okay then, you heartless little monster, we’ll
step up our game with SCP-3017.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:02.760
Now, you might be forgiven for thinking that
this is a particularly monstrous SCP, after

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.590
all, that is his anomalous effect.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:12.060
Fraisure Melbrook is – or rather, was – an
otherwise ordinary man afflicted with an infectious

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.670
cognitohazard.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:17.830
This would alter the perception of anyone
around Fraisure or any person who might have

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:22.480
spoken to him, and made them believe that
he belonged in custody.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.760
People would be compelled to think that Fraisure
was a dangerous, violent individual, but with

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.810
no actual proof that he was.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:34.750
In many cases, anyone exposed to his cognitohazard
would even try to lock him up themselves.

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:38.830
The one reprieve, however, was that a number
of Fraisure’s immediate family and close

00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:41.350
friends were immune to this effect.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:46.980
Either this was through experiencing prolonged
exposure to SCP-3017, or by simply knowing

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:52.030
him well enough to have context that canceled
out the false beliefs that he was a violent

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.370
criminal with an extensive record.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:04:01.060
Designated as SCP-3017-1s, the people closest
to him remained unaffected, and Fraisure was

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:06.920
able to maintain healthy relationships with
all of them… until SCP-3017 fell into the

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.250
hands of the Foundation.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:14.769
Arrested and placed in containment, thanks
to the anomalous effect of SCP-3017, the Foundation

00:04:14.769 --> 00:04:18.870
believed that Fraisure Melbrook had close
ties and connections to multiple Groups of

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:19.870
Interest.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:25.070
They began to relentlessly, torturously interrogate
Fraisure, threatening his family unless he

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:30.860
gave them the answers to their questions;
information that he simply could not provide.

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:36.180
He hadn’t even heard of the GOIs the Foundation
wanted to know about, let alone had any direct

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.889
affiliation with them.

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:41.970
But still, they kept pressing him, keeping
him locked up and not allowing him to see

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:45.930
his family even when his grandmother developed
lung cancer.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:52.620
Treated with suspicion and hostility non-stop
every day, SCP-3017 would get one last shot

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:54.250
at freedom, however.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:59.100
Researcher Rylan, who had been studying Fraisure’s
anomalous properties, as well as a security

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.460
officer that had been assigned to watch Melbrook,
both developed the same immunity to the prisoner’s

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.669
cognitohazard as his family and friends had.

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:13.169
Working together, they were able to spring
SCP-3017 from Foundation custody, and Rylan

00:05:13.169 --> 00:05:15.490
took him back home to see his family.

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:19.039
Little did either of them know of the tragedy
that awaited them.

00:05:19.039 --> 00:05:23.979
In a cruel twist of fate, a house fire had
set Melbrook’s family home ablaze, claiming

00:05:23.979 --> 00:05:30.100
the lives of his parents, both brothers, his
sister, grandmother, and even his fiancé.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:34.810
Utterly devastated and overwhelmed with grief,
the loss of his loved ones completely broke

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:35.810
Fraisure.

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:40.850
He had spent so much of his life persecuted,
accused of heinous crimes and arrested despite

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:42.729
being completely innocent.

00:05:42.729 --> 00:05:47.460
And as if that wasn’t bad enough, he’d
fought his way free from the Foundation, only

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:51.790
to find his home reduced to ash the day before
he made it back.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:56.650
By the time the SCP Foundation arrived to
re-capture him, it was already too late.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:01.270
Fraisure was dead and Researcher Rylan turned
herself over without resistance.

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:05.520
The Foundation soon realized what they had
done, how they had tortured an innocent man

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.139
who didn’t actually have the information
they thought he did.

00:06:09.139 --> 00:06:15.840
In quiet shame, they reclassified SCP-3017
under a new object class: ‘Neutralised’.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.060
Feeling teary yet?

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:18.800
Maybe a little moist around the eyes?

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.500
Oh, those are just your allergies?

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:24.710
Of course they are, how could we be so silly.

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.189
Alright, let’s move on.

00:06:27.189 --> 00:06:31.930
Taking a step back from the grim, dark reality
for a moment, let’s instead delve into a

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.430
world of fantasy.

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:40.810
SCP-1762 was a seemingly ordinary carboard
box, spray-painted silver with the words ‘Here

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.759
Be Dragons’ written on it in permanent marker.

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:49.450
Usually, it remained empty, but would occasionally
open, releasing dozens of tiny origami dragons

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:53.310
that would fly out after black smoke had plumed
out of the box.

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:58.040
It was no doubt a sight to behold, the kind
of fantastical occurrence that is inherently

00:06:58.040 --> 00:06:59.720
filled with childlike wonder.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.730
Often, a written or carved message would be
left behind after the origami dragons flew

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:09.580
back into their box; and this is how the Foundation
was able to learn of the terrible misfortune

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:12.250
known as ‘The Jabberwocky Event.’

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:17.630
The paper dragons existed in a world of Fantasy,
all contained within the cardboard box.

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:21.979
When they first emerged, the message they
left greeted someone who had seemingly created

00:07:21.979 --> 00:07:28.310
the box and its fantastical contents, or had
at least interacted with it before; possibly

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:33.690
a particularly imaginative child with the
reality-bending capability to give life to

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.860
the inanimate paper dragons.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:40.650
The origami creatures seemed to mistake the
Foundation for this original child rediscovering

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:43.580
them after an extended period of time.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:47.830
They made mention of the existence of other
mystical beings in their world, including

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:51.140
Giants, Elves, Centaurs, and more.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:56.440
Through their subsequent messages, it appeared
something about the world of SCP-1762 was

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:58.560
gradually becoming unstable, though.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.830
It required imagination to sustain it, to
stabilize the Fantasy within the box.

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:10.180
But unfortunately, the Foundation personnel
examining SCP-1762 refused to accept the existence

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:11.410
of this world.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:17.120
They instead leaned on science, cold, hard
facts, not the whimsy of the imaginary.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:22.909
When opening the box, all they saw was its
empty interior, so to them, the Fantasy wasn’t

00:08:22.909 --> 00:08:23.909
real.

00:08:23.909 --> 00:08:28.460
It was the SCP Foundation’s own lack of
belief, their refusal to make-believe, that

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:33.640
was gradually killing the world inside SCP-1762.

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:38.020
Through their notes, still unaware that they
weren’t speaking with their child creator,

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.330
the origami dragons pleaded for aid.

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:43.390
Cries that fell on the Foundation’s deaf
ears.

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:48.360
Within the box, a magical war broke out between
the various creatures inside, with the survivors

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.260
fleeing for their lives.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:56.029
The box itself was effectively a portal, linking
reality with this realm of Fantasy.

00:08:56.029 --> 00:09:02.079
It existed in a time when the myths and legends
of old were indeterminable from fact.

00:09:02.079 --> 00:09:09.131
SCP-1762 had once represented a physical bond,
a relationship between those two worlds, but

00:09:09.131 --> 00:09:14.850
as time had moved on, belief in legends, in
Fantasy, had all but dwindled.

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:19.500
As the world inside the box fell into chaos,
becoming more and more unstable, the words

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:23.130
‘Here Be Dragons’ were burned away from
the carboard surface.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:28.040
In their final message, the dragons mentioned
they hoped to escape to a new world, concluding

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:29.950
with:
“We are sad.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:31.040
We love you.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:32.670
We will not Forget you.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:33.670
We are scared.

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:35.510
Will You Forget Us?”

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:40.529
Soon after, the box returned to its original
state, with the phrase ‘Here Were Dragons’

00:09:40.529 --> 00:09:46.560
on the side, marking the end of the Jabberwocky
Event, and potentially, the Fantasy within

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.610
SCP-1762, forever.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:54.810
From mystical, magical tales of sadness, we
now turn to a sad story from within the ranks

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.380
of the Foundation itself.

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:02.810
SCP-451, formerly a field agent working in
the Foundation, seems to an outside observer

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.810
to be little more than the average man in
his thirties.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:09.029
Well, as average as someone working for the
Foundation can be.

00:10:09.029 --> 00:10:14.730
But let’s turn things around, if we were
to look from SCP-451’s perspective, how

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:15.730
would he see us?

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:21.720
That’s just the thing… he wouldn’t,
he can’t see us, or anyone else for that

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:22.720
matter.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:25.840
You see, for a time, this agent was declared
missing in action by the SCP Foundation.

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:31.400
The last recorded assignment he was sent on
was an attempt to retrieve a dangerous anomalous

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:32.400
artifact.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.639
However, this attempt ultimately failed, and
SCP-451 resurfaced a month later at Site 19.

00:10:38.639 --> 00:10:43.170
Although, from his point of view, the entire
Foundation facility was empty.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:48.160
He was the last man on Earth, or so he thought,
at least.

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:52.970
Some unknown influence had permanently altered
the agent’s perception; he had been rendered

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:55.910
incapable of perceiving other human beings.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:11:00.750
Now, if you’re a bit of a hermit who prefers
their own company, then this might initially

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:02.160
sound like heaven.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:06.500
Never having to experience another awkward
interaction with another person; like when

00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:10.170
you’re walking down the street, and someone’s
walking towards you, then you have to try

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:13.610
to figure out which way they’re going to
go so you can step in the opposite direction

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:15.510
and avoid bumping into them.

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:19.050
Then you inevitably to that weird shuffle
when you both go to walk the same way.

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:20.190
It’s a whole thing.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:25.029
Well, as good as it might sound to be rid
of happenings like that, imagine if you couldn’t

00:11:25.029 --> 00:11:26.980
see or hear anyone.

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:31.750
Any attempts the rest of the world made to
communicate with you, not one of them registering.

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:34.450
But it was somehow worse than that.

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:39.810
Thanks to his newfound surroundings, or lack
thereof, SCP-451 believed that by interacting

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:45.320
with the anomalous artifact, he had single-handedly
wiped out the entire human race.

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:51.410
The guilt of that action, although only true
from his perspective, weighed heavily on SCP-451’s

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:52.570
conscience.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:58.079
He spent a lot of time wandering the corridors
of Site 19, overwhelmed by his apparent isolation.

00:11:58.079 --> 00:12:02.720
Eventually, the loneliness of his newfound
condition, coupled with the fallacious knowledge

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:07.589
that he had killed the entire human race,
drove him to a state of near madness.

00:12:07.589 --> 00:12:11.220
But there would be no escape from this new
state of perpetual hell.

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:16.020
Not only did the condition appear to be incurable,
at least from what he was capable of doing,

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:19.089
but he would not even find solace in death.

00:12:19.089 --> 00:12:23.399
He had been cursed with some kind of sadistic
immortality, and it seemed like the only way

00:12:23.399 --> 00:12:27.880
to ever connect with others was when they
themselves were near death.

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:31.000
As for what the Foundation were doing to help
SCP-451?

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:32.640
Well, nothing.

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:35.770
There’s little that can do to reverse the
change.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:39.540
He was a valuable field agent to them before
it happened, and it’s their hope that he

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:43.220
can be again if the process is somehow reversed.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:48.309
Until then, SCP-451 is forced to live out
this painful existence, spending every day

00:12:48.309 --> 00:12:51.579
– as far as he can tell – alone.

00:12:51.579 --> 00:12:56.389
Of course, if SCP-451 could wish for anything
in his predicament, it might be something

00:12:56.389 --> 00:12:58.190
as simple as some company.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:13:04.090
If only there was an SCP that granted the
user their ultimate wish, fulfilled any fantasy,

00:13:04.090 --> 00:13:06.500
made them the hero of their own story.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:10.589
Well, that happens to be the function of SCP-1230.

00:13:10.589 --> 00:13:15.570
It appears as a green, hardcover book with
no discernible labels on the spine, nor on

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:17.730
either front or back covers.

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:23.529
The pages are almost entirely blacnk, save
for one simple phrase on the first one: ‘A

00:13:23.529 --> 00:13:25.650
hero is born.’

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:31.519
Anyone that reads this page of SCP-1230 won’t
experience any effects immediately, in fact,

00:13:31.519 --> 00:13:35.149
its anomalous properties don’t become apparent
until the reader falls asleep.

00:13:35.149 --> 00:13:39.709
When they do, they will experience a vivid
dream that can last anywhere from forty-five

00:13:39.709 --> 00:13:44.329
seconds to… well, let’s just say considerably
longer.

00:13:44.329 --> 00:13:48.279
Although the reader will never be asleep for
any longer than they normally would be.

00:13:48.279 --> 00:13:52.980
In their dream, the reader will be the protagonist
within their own fantasy world.

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:57.980
The entire time, they remain fully aware of
their surroundings, their senses and faculties

00:13:57.980 --> 00:14:00.980
all work as they would as if they were awake.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:06.820
Not unlike the Fantasy world of SCP-1762,
the dream worlds experienced by test subjects

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:12.000
who read SCP-1230 vary depending on the imagination
of the dreamer.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:16.079
Additionally, once they re-emerge from their
slumber, they will be able to recall all the

00:14:16.079 --> 00:14:19.760
events of the dream down to every last detail.

00:14:19.760 --> 00:14:25.380
Every dream induced by reading SCP-1230 will
also feature a character known only as the

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:31.079
Book Keeper, a friendly, bearded old man in
a green cloak who is apparently the personification

00:14:31.079 --> 00:14:32.540
of the book itself.

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:36.420
The role of the Book Keeper seems to be to
create the ‘fantasy-scapes’ as he calls

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:42.230
them, and seemingly creates these bespoke
worlds on behalf of all the dreamers he encounters,

00:14:42.230 --> 00:14:45.690
in accordance with their own deepest desires
and wants.

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:50.060
By now, you’re no doubt thinking, how could
this possibly be a sad SCP?

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:54.500
I bet you probably even want a go with SCP-1230
yourself, don’t you?

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:56.139
And who wouldn’t?

00:14:56.139 --> 00:15:00.680
After all, you would get to be placed in a
fantasy-scape where you can do anything and

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.600
everything you can think of.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.540
On top of that, this isn’t Inception.

00:15:04.540 --> 00:15:09.500
Dying or being harmed in one of these dreams
doesn’t translate to real-world death…

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:11.690
well, not directly anyway.

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:16.019
Remember when we mentioned the dreams can
last a ‘considerably long’ time; at least

00:15:16.019 --> 00:15:18.110
in their own, internal fantasy-scape time.

00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:23.880
Well, here’s what makes it one of the saddest
SCPs on this list, maybe even ever.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:28.290
Imagine getting to live out a fantasy adventure
as the hero, the main protagonist in your

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:29.709
own story.

00:15:29.709 --> 00:15:34.250
Picture you spent a number of years in that
dream time, experiencing every event of that

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.810
story, that world firsthand.

00:15:36.810 --> 00:15:40.930
It looks real, it feels real; for the time
that you’re there, at least.

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:43.130
So, what happens when you wake up?

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:47.590
How are you going to feel if you’re all
of a sudden pulled out of that fantasy and

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:50.810
dropped back into the mundanity of real life?

00:15:50.810 --> 00:15:52.980
Would you prefer your fantasy to the real
world?

00:15:52.980 --> 00:15:56.010
And what would it do to you if you couldn’t
go back?

00:15:56.010 --> 00:16:00.240
This was experienced firsthand by one of the
Foundation’s researchers, known only as

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:05.760
Professor B. He had specifically requested
the Foundation to allow him to read SCP-1230,

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:09.950
and was giddy with excitement when it came
to be his turn.

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:14.610
Like many others, he read the first page,
seeing ‘A hero is born’ and then instantly

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:19.970
went to sleep, unwilling to wait until later,
clearly eager to get experience his very own

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:21.519
fantasy-scape.

00:16:21.519 --> 00:16:25.920
Professor B was asleep for the next twenty-four
hours before the Foundation’s security personnel

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.850
were forced to attempt to wake him up.

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:33.110
When they did, he looked around, confused
to be unceremoniously returned to real life.

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:37.360
It was almost as if the amount of time he
had spent within the Book Keeper’s dream

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:41.470
world had caused him to momentarily forget
his old life with the Foundation.

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:46.290
However, he soon regained his memory after
a brief reminder from fellow staff.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.440
But it was never the same after that.

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:54.000
Professor B had experienced the life he always
dreamed of within his dreams, and upon waking,

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:59.610
he had been returned to a life where he was
an aging, tired man in a dead-end job at the

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:00.610
Foundation.

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:03.600
He was no hero here. he was just another nobody.

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:06.530
And the sadness was too much for him.

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:09.150
Professor B passed away not long after.

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:11.939
After this tragedy, the Book Keeper was utterly
distraught.

00:17:11.939 --> 00:17:15.980
Through the medium of the book, he left a
note for the personnel of the SCP Foundation.

00:17:15.980 --> 00:17:18.250
It read:
“I’m so sorry.

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:20.530
I never intended for this to happen.

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:22.970
I just wanted to make people happy.”

00:17:22.970 --> 00:17:26.820
A number of weeks passed before the Foundation
was able to make contact with the Book Keeper

00:17:26.820 --> 00:17:27.820
again.

00:17:27.820 --> 00:17:32.330
Leaving a note that requested a conversation
with the entity, a Foundation doctor experienced

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:35.280
a dream that was set in a dark, featureless
void.

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.820
There, he encountered the Book Keeper, crying
to himself.

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:43.770
When questioned about what had happened to
Professor B, the embodiment of SCP-1230 explained:

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:46.750
“He had such an active imagination!

00:17:46.750 --> 00:17:51.880
I was able to create a vast and beautiful
universe for him and it was obvious that he

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:54.470
had wanted a life like that for so long.

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:57.470
He conquered foul beasts and rescued princesses…

00:17:57.470 --> 00:18:02.500
He built kingdoms and even raised a family…
but he never wanted to leave.

00:18:02.500 --> 00:18:07.039
He delved so far into his fantasy world that
I soon realized he preferred his dream over

00:18:07.039 --> 00:18:08.330
the real world.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:13.179
I reminded him that this was all merely an
illusion, but he wouldn’t listen to me.

00:18:13.179 --> 00:18:16.770
I tried to keep him happy for as long as I
could…”

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:20.250
Remember when we mentioned the dreams can
last a ‘considerably long’ time?

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:23.559
(At least in their own, internal fantasy-scape
time).

00:18:23.559 --> 00:18:29.520
Professor B had been living out his ultimate
fantasy adventure for two-hundred years, while

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:33.160
only a full day’s worth of time had passed
in the real world.

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:37.620
Having to live through all that, only to wake
up and realize none of it was real was perhaps

00:18:37.620 --> 00:18:40.350
too existentially overwhelming for the Professor.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:45.020
Then again, maybe he had just preferred the
world of his fantasy-scape much more than

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:46.130
the world around him.

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:50.630
And finally, that brings us to one of the
most infamous anomalies in the Foundation’s

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:52.080
extensive history.

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:58.600
SCP-096, better known as the Shy Guy, is a
terrifying, towering creature with a hairless

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:02.220
body, sunken eyes and a huge, gaping mouth.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:06.390
But don’t you ever get the sense that there’s
something inherently…

00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:09.289
sad about SCP-096?

00:19:09.289 --> 00:19:13.760
Insecurity is something a lot of us experience,
and have to face every day.

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:17.450
But the Shy Guy’s is taken to such an extreme
degree.

00:19:17.450 --> 00:19:20.289
He can’t bear the thought of anyone looking
at him.

00:19:20.289 --> 00:19:25.040
It’s so bad for him, causes him to experience
so much distress that he has to travel lighting

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:28.201
fast to anyone that looks at his face.

00:19:28.201 --> 00:19:32.570
Once he’s caught up with them and killed
them, then he’s right back to his usual

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:36.720
routine: sobbing and weeping, or pacing around
his cell.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:40.770
Perhaps his story isn’t as sad as some of
the others on this list, but we do know it’d

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:46.380
be a welcome change if someone could make
the Shy Guy smile, just for a little while.

00:19:46.380 --> 00:19:52.260
Now go check out “25 MOST FRIGHTENING SCPS”
and “WHAT’S THE SCARIEST SCP?”

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:55.730
if you’d like to replace that sadness with
some good, old-fashioned fear!

